WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.292
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:08.500
AARON SMALL: Welcome to
"Kubernetes Security The Easy

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.040
Way."

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.350
My name's Aaron Small,
and also, today, we'll

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.010
have Greg and CJ, software
engineers on the GKE security

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.800
team.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.890
Ostensibly, we're here to
talk about GKE security,

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.200
but really this
talk is about trust.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.530
When you choose GKE or any cloud
provider to host your product,

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.900
you're placing your
trust in them, in us.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.390
And I don't know about you,
but I can't open the news

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:34.660
without reading some headline
about information security,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.330
some cyber attack,
or the latest breach

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.560
of sensitive information,
which, to those people impacted,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.910
is just one more
breach of trust.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.500
Software security
is something we all

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.530
have to care about
today, but to many

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.570
it can feel like a
distraction from doing what's

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.180
most important, which
is building your product

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.982
and delighting your customers.

00:00:53.982 --> 00:00:55.690
We're passionate about
software security.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.060
We get up every day
delighted by the prospect

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.730
of finding that vulnerability
before the bad guys do

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.140
and building the
tools people can

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.140
use to build state of the
art, secure applications.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.520
There are lots of
talks and blogs

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.770
and white papers you can
read that will talk about how

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.600
to harden your
Kubernetes cluster,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.320
talk about common attacks, or
highlight recent compromises.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.210
This talk is not about that.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.130
Like I said, this
talk is about trust.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.350
And in my experience, the
best way to build trust

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:21.910
is to be transparent.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.160
So we're giving you a
backstage pass, into the work

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.610
we do at Google, behind the
scenes, under embargoes,

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.530
and even before you build
your first container

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.790
to keep your cluster
as secure as possible.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.980
For those of you who
are familiar with GKE,

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.470
it's not unlikely that you
think of it as simply a managed

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.150
Kubernetes control
plane, and you

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.340
might infer from that that
the security we provide

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.830
stops there.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.660
While we do take control plane
security quite seriously,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.310
that's not the end of it.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.160
Greg and CJ will discuss
how we secure your GKE

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.410
nodes, the control
plane, and the tools

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.390
that we've built that
you have at your disposal

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.600
for attack detection and
vulnerability mitigation.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.550
CJ also has a demo
that'll demonstrate

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.460
a potential compromise scenario
and how the tools we built

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.240
make it easy to
respond and mitigate.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.490
OK, most of you have probably
seen a diagram like this

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.310
before.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.600
So please forgive the
early morning GKE 101.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.550
I do want to give a
little bit of groundwork,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.040
though, for the rest
of the presentation.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.460
GKE, in the simplest terms, is a
hosted Kubernetes control plane

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.550
and some number of
Kubernetes nodes.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.200
You, the developers
and administrators,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.720
interact with the control
plane and your users interact

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.510
with the containers that
you've deployed on the nodes.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.830
The control plane
manages your workloads.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:37.270
It manages what nodes belong to
the cluster and the certificate

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.670
authority that
handles authentication

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.160
of the node agents,
also called kubelets.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.070
The nodes themselves
execute containers,

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.230
receive configuration
data, secrets, et cetera,

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.313
that they need to function
from the control plane.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.710
It's on those nodes where
the aforementioned hardening

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.112
guides and best
practices come into play.

00:02:58.112 --> 00:02:59.820
As you get more familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.180
you'll customize this and
configure it and really make it

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:02.680
your own.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.820
It can get as complex and as
unique as you want to make it.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.860
The security of this
phase follows the shared

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.140
responsibility
model, meaning we're

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.900
responsible for the security
of the infrastructure, itself,

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.800
and you're responsible
for what you deploy there.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.650
You manage, among other
things, the node specification,

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.830
which has all the standard
VM knobs you might expect,

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.820
number of cores, amount of
RAM, the operating system,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.240
whether or not there's
a GPU attached,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.330
what services and workloads
you've deployed there,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.259
and any Google Cloud
Service accounts

00:03:35.259 --> 00:03:37.425
that you use to talk to
other Google Cloud Services.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.740
But today we're talking about
what Google does for you.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.560
So with that, I want to
hand off to Greg, who's

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.500
going to discuss node security.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.260
GREG CASTLE: Thanks, Aaron.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.950
So I'm going to start out the
behind the scenes tour talking

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.989
about node security.

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:04.530
I'm going to build this picture
up in a few different layers.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.640
So we're going to start at
the VM provisioning layer.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.160
There's been a number of other
Google talks, in the past,

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.750
about the great
security work Google's

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.150
doing below that layer, things
like hardware and firmware

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.290
security.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:18.350
I'm going to skip that in
favor of covering new material

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.589
and making sure we get
to Kubernetes stuff.

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:24.980
So starting at the virtual
machine layer, then operating

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.420
system on top, then the kubelet,
and how we bootstrap the trust

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:33.140
in that system, and
finally, the system

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.720
containers that make up
Kubernetes and other Google

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.290
containers that we run.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:42.270
So starting at the VM
provisioning layer.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.910
So when you create
a cluster with GKE,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.520
you get nodes that are
automatically provisioned

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.360
into a virtual private cloud.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.180
We create a firewall that
has just enough privilege

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.070
for the control plane
to be able to talk

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:58.620
to the nodes and the nodes to
be able to talk to each other.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.740
The VMs have discs that
are encrypted at rest.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.590
You can read all about
that at this link here.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.800
And they really benefit
from a lot of work

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.570
that Google's done over the
years to harden our hypervisor.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:14.130
So Google has spent a large
amount of security time

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:18.255
making sure that VMs running
on that same shared hardware

00:05:18.255 --> 00:05:20.520
are really well isolated
from each other,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.400
and our VMs inherit all that
great work that Google's

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.170
done previously.

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:30.490
As an optional feature-- at the
moment it's currently beta--

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.580
when you create a cluster, you
can make it a private cluster.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.310
And that means that
you essentially take it

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:37.550
off the public internet.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.720
So your nodes get private IP
addresses, RFC 1918 addresses.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.509
And whenever you do
that, it's a good--

00:05:45.509 --> 00:05:47.800
I think, when you don't need
a service on the internet,

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.258
it's always good to be able to
remove it from the internet.

00:05:50.258 --> 00:05:54.520
You kind of take out a bunch
of attacks related to just

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.690
general internet scanning.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.230
So that's the virtual
machine layer.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.300
So the next layer up is
the operating system.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:05.380
So the default operating
system that we offer on GKE

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.540
is the Container-Optimized OS.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.930
We also offer
Ubuntu by Canonical.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.830
But in the interest
of time today, I'm

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.500
just focusing on COS.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.300
Canonical also has a
great security team

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:20.020
and builds a great OS, but
I'm talking about COS today.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.620
So this is an OS
that's built by Google.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.000
It's based on Chromium OS.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.590
It's a really minimal OS.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:29.200
It's really just enough
OS to run containers

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.320
and not much else.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.010
And that's good,
because less code

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.220
means less vulnerabilities,
less attack surface, less

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.360
maintenance burden,
less patching.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:41.736
And all of that stuff is good.

00:06:41.736 --> 00:06:43.610
We've also spent a
significant amount of time

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:45.150
hardening this operating system.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.720
So we've enabled a whole bunch
of different kernel security

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:52.550
features and set a
bunch of secure defaults

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:56.150
at the kernel layer and various
other operating system knobs.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:58.760
And the idea there is to
make that initial execution

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.706
step of any attack much harder.

00:07:00.706 --> 00:07:02.330
So if we can put in
a memory protection

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.520
in the kernel that makes
it harder for attackers

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:07.390
to execute, that's a win.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.740
We've also spent
quite a bit of time

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.642
trying to make it
harder to persist

00:07:11.642 --> 00:07:13.350
through a number of
different mechanisms.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.560
So things like an
immutable root file

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.840
system, making the
root system read-only,

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:19.550
and having a verified
boot chain means

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.680
that a number of
those traditional ways

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:24.020
to persist on an
operating system

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:28.190
are made much harder on COS.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:31.250
We're also enabling
features like eBPF

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.830
to allow third party security
products, particularly

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:35.480
detection products,
to run on GKE.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:39.170
We want those products
to work well on GKE.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.900
And there's more work
you can do here if you

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:43.080
want to go the extra mile.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.610
So COS also supports
seccomp and apparmor,

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:52.220
so you can really write
a very specific hardening

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:53.850
for your particular application.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.640
If you'd like to go
that far into it,

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:59.600
you can define the normal
behavior of your application.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.130
And the idea there is that,
if an attacker is trying

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.550
to operate outside
the bounds of what

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.680
is normal for your
application, then

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.480
it should be blocked
by these technologies

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.410
and make their
life a lot harder.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.850
And you can read more about
COS and all the hardening

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.580
and security work we're
doing there at this address.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.010
And for all these
like little links

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:21.900
that I'm firing up
on the screen here,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.900
I have a giant slide at
the end with a big list.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:29.525
So if you just want to take
a photo of that one, you can.

00:08:29.525 --> 00:08:32.210
I also briefly wanted to
mention the team behind COS.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:36.140
So we have a dedicated team
that is working on maintenance,

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.440
testing, security patching.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.200
We also have a lot of
Google in-house Linux kernel

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.250
expertise, so that benefits
the whole Linux ecosystem.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:50.210
Those people are developing
new kernel security features

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.730
and also finding vulnerabilities
and patching them,

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:57.110
but we're also benefiting
from that expertise with COS.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.720
So we're driving features
through the kernel and into COS

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.010
that we're making use of.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.300
So I'll just give you a
second to absorb this diagram.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:08.690
We're up to the next level now.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:12.590
So we've got VM, OS, and then
on top of that is Kubernetes.

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.780
So this is a fairly
simplified version

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:18.380
of how the kubelet
bootstraps its trust.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.210
So we're going to
walk through this

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.980
and give you an idea
of how that happens.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.580
So what makes a Kubernetes
node a Kubernetes node?

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:27.350
It's when you have
a kubelet that's

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:30.530
registered with the API server
and can authenticate to it.

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:35.730
So we start down the bottom of
this picture with the node VM,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:39.260
where the kubelet's running,
and it retrieves a bootstrap key

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:41.660
from the metadata server,
which is running outside the VM

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:43.680
but beside it.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.220
So it retrieves that
key and then creates

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.940
a Certificate Signing Request--

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.580
it's a standard X.509
Certificate Signing Request--

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.160
and sends that to
the API server.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.880
The API server receives
that CSR and checks

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.550
that it's authenticated
with that bootstrap key.

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:04.780
It does some other
checks on the CSR itself,

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:08.491
and then it sends it off, via
a webhook, to a signing API.

00:10:08.491 --> 00:10:10.490
And that's actually running
outside the cluster.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:14.150
So that hosted control plane
is running on a GCE VM,

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.090
and the Google prod box is--

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:20.480
you may have heard of Borg,
our internal infrastructure.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.180
So that's signing APIs.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:24.890
And keys are held externally.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.770
So that call goes
out to that API,

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:29.200
the certificate
request gets signed,

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.162
and the cert propagates
back to the kubelet.

00:10:32.162 --> 00:10:33.620
The interesting
part of all of this

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.270
is that the signing
keys are actually

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.167
held externally to the cluster.

00:10:37.167 --> 00:10:39.500
So we have an extra trust
boundary in between those two.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.840
Properties of this procedure.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:49.040
The traffic is all
authenticated and encrypted.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.530
The trust comes from
the virtual machine.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.230
We end up with a per node
X.509 identity, which

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:55.700
is actually really important.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.500
CJ will talk later
about how this

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.870
is useful in control
plane security

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.770
with the node authorizer.

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:05.460
I mentioned the CA
is off the cluster.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:08.600
And in the future, we're
looking at how we can improve

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.010
this bootstrapping process.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:14.240
And in Kubernetes 1.11, my
team contributed some code

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.580
that will allow the kubelet
to bootstrap itself using

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:18.170
a trusted platform module.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:21.200
And the idea there
is that the kubelet

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.059
will no longer have to handle
the key material directly.

00:11:24.059 --> 00:11:25.350
So that will be a security win.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.750
So once we have a
kubelet, that isn't all

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:33.070
that we need for the cluster.

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:36.140
There's a bunch of other cluster
services that need to run.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:40.840
So there's Kubernetes things,
like kube-proxy, cluster DNS,

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.120
and there's also
Google extra add-ons,

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.930
like logging containers,
monitoring containers, as well

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.270
as things like a
container runtime.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.362
So Google does a lot of
work to maintain these, make

00:11:52.362 --> 00:11:54.820
sure they are working, make
sure they work with the control

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:58.210
plane, and make sure
they scale appropriately.

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.100
But the security-relevant
pieces are

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:03.310
that we deliver these in a
defensible configuration,

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.950
that you wouldn't find things
like a container runtime

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:09.530
port listening on the open
internet, for example.

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:13.210
We also look at the security of
all these containers regularly,

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:16.750
so we're scanning the containers
referenced in the Kubernetes

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.390
source code for
vulnerabilities and we're

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:21.780
patching those where
patches are missing.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.720
So that's kind of what we
do with those containers.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.730
There's actually some work
here for you, the GKE user,

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.900
to do with these
containers, as well.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:37.920
So you really need to be
able to upgrade your nodes

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:38.880
to get those patches.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.000
So I just talked about the
security patching process.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.955
Essentially, how that works is
we will patch the container,

00:12:45.955 --> 00:12:47.580
release a new version
of the container,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.280
and then update Kubernetes
to reference that new version

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:51.480
of the container.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.640
And when we do that, we'll
bump the node version.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:57.510
So that a node upgrade is
essentially like the rising

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.340
tide that lifts the
container ship that

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.380
has all the containers on it.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.662
So to make that
process easier, there's

00:13:03.662 --> 00:13:05.370
actually an auto
upgrade feature that you

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:08.250
can opt into, so
you can say, Google,

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:11.320
please handle the upgrades of
these nodes, automatically.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:12.930
I don't want to think about it.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.480
And to give you some control
over when that happens,

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.400
you can actually specify
a maintenance window.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.640
So that's a four-hour window
that says to Google, hey,

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:22.710
please do your upgrade,
your maintenance tasks

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:23.610
within this window.

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:27.940
So that's node security.

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:30.520
We talked about VM
provisioning, in terms

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:33.850
of hypervisor, firewalls,
the operating system, kernel

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:37.390
hardening, security patching,
Kubernetes trust bootstrap,

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:40.660
in terms of how the CA works,
and the system containers

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:43.390
and how they're
patched and upgraded.

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:46.780
The takeaway I'd like you
to take, from this section,

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:49.060
is that Google's doing a
whole bunch of work here,

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.190
so that you don't have to
care about any of this,

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.122
and you can focus
on your application.

00:13:53.122 --> 00:13:54.580
But the one thing
you do need to do

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:58.340
is look after your
node upgrades.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:13:59.860
So I'm going to
hand it over to CJ

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:01.762
to talk about control
plane security.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.420
CJ CULLEN: Thanks, Greg.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:14.210
So Greg gave us a
good overview of what

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:18.020
GKE provides you in terms of
the managed node environment.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:20.920
But a lot of what you
think of, of the security

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.571
that GKE provides you, is the
managed Kubernetes control

00:14:24.571 --> 00:14:25.070
plane.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:33.660
So on GKE, each
cluster control plane

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:37.200
is separated, by a VM boundary,
from every other cluster

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.970
control plane.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:40.860
So that means that
the API server

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.500
that you talk to
is entirely yours,

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:47.340
the etcd database that houses
your Kubernetes API resources

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.110
is all yours, and
then the add-ons

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.230
that run in your
cluster are all yours.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.900
The Kubernetes API
that you talk to

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:58.810
is the same Kubernetes that you
would get running it yourself.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.980
But on GKE, that's
all managed by Google.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:07.000
So a big part of that isolation
is the per cluster certificate

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.050
authority.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:11.020
Greg mentioned it, that
governs the node credentials

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.050
that the node agents use to
authenticate to the managed

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:15.430
control plane.

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:18.730
It also governs the
server side credentials

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:22.130
that the managed control point
authenticates itself with.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.220
The key for that,
as Greg mentioned,

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:26.620
is held off the cluster
for defense in depth,

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.390
encrypted at rest.

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:30.640
The fact that it
is a per cluster CA

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:33.580
means that we get a
simplified chain of trust.

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:38.290
We don't depend on a public
CA or PKI for this to work.

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:41.200
And then that key is
rotatable on-demand,

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:43.155
either just for good
hygiene or as part

00:15:43.155 --> 00:15:44.155
of an incident response.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.880
So Greg mentioned
the per node identity

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.880
that you get as part of that
kubelet trust bootstrap.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:54.490
And that per node identity
becomes super important

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:57.820
when we start thinking about how
nodes interact with the managed

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:00.810
control plane to
manage what workloads

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.290
they're actually running.

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:05.290
So node agents have
only the permissions

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:07.400
they need to do their job.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:09.220
So that means that
one node is only

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.760
allowed to update
status about itself,

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:14.320
and it's only allowed
to see things,

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:16.150
like secrets and
config maps, that

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:18.910
are actually pertinent
to the workloads that

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:20.890
are scheduled onto it.

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:22.830
And this is just another
defense in depth step

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:27.460
that helps make a compromise
of one node stay limited

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:28.360
to just that node.

00:16:31.615 --> 00:16:33.990
So another huge benefit you
get, from the managed control

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:37.020
plane that GKE gives you, is
the seamless authentication

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.450
with the same user
accounts that you already

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:41.250
have on Google Cloud.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.220
So whether you are taking
a look at usage or digging

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.680
through logs in
the Cloud console,

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.020
or you're on the command line
debugging your Kubernetes

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.140
application using G
Cloud and kube control,

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.575
you're the same user with the
same strong authentication

00:16:54.575 --> 00:16:56.450
that you get anywhere
else from Google Cloud.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.610
Building on top of that
authentication story,

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:06.510
GKE is integrated with
Google IAM, which gives you

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.094
the ability to manage
the permissions,

00:17:09.094 --> 00:17:11.010
grant your team just the
permissions they need

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:13.710
to do their job, and
then see and enforce

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:17.960
that policy in one place across
your entire organization.

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.599
For advanced Kubernetes
users, this all

00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:23.089
plays nice with Kubernetes
RBAC for access control.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:29.950
So now that you know who is
supposed to be able to do what,

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:31.780
it's still really
helpful to have

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:33.190
that audit trail,
after the fact,

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:35.240
to see what actually happened.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:38.350
So the pertinent
info you want on GKE

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:42.130
is what has accessed
the Kubernetes API

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:44.140
and also what operations
have taken place

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:45.670
on the underlying GKE clusters.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:51.760
So if you've ever been
in a situation where

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:55.900
you had an incident, and then
you looked after the fact, just

00:17:55.900 --> 00:18:01.040
to realize that you forgot to
turn on logs, well, on GCP,

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.340
we give you 400
days of retention,

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:07.220
of what we call admin
activity logs, by default.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.050
So that's mutating operations.

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:13.360
So on GKE, that's things like
creating new Kubernetes pods

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:17.980
or upgrading the version
of your GKE nodes.

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:21.040
For everything else, for
things like read access

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:25.270
to the Kubernetes API, you
can enable data access logs

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:28.760
and those get
retained for 30 days.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.900
Those get ingested directly
to Stackdriver, where

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:33.130
you can analyze them there.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:35.032
If you have another
tool that you

00:18:35.032 --> 00:18:36.490
like to analyze
your logs with, you

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:39.760
can easily export logs from
Stackdriver using something

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:42.910
like Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:46.780
So to show off how powerful
all of these features

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:50.104
can be together, I'm going to
set up a little demo scenario.

00:18:50.104 --> 00:18:51.520
So we're going to
say that we have

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:53.950
this continuous integration,
continuous deployment

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:56.740
system, that, every
hour, it's going

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:58.870
to build a new version
of our application

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:02.530
and it's going to then
push that application,

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:04.930
artifacts up to
Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:06.610
and then once that's
done, we're going

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:08.530
to deploy the new version
of our application

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:12.570
out to our GKE cluster
for some testing.

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:13.390
So that's all good.

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:14.765
We've got that
running, and then,

00:19:14.765 --> 00:19:17.740
one day, we get
the dreaded email.

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:21.490
Google has detected that some
credential, from our project,

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.290
appears to have been
exposed somewhere

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:25.150
on the public internet.

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:29.080
We dig back a little bit, and
sure enough, we accidentally

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:34.340
checked in our private key to
a public source control repo.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:36.790
So what that means is
that the deploy piece

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:42.730
of this CI/CD system we set up
is now using a credential that

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:45.400
has access to a GKE
cluster, and that credential

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:47.890
may be exposed somewhere.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:49.630
So how do we respond to this?

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:52.094
So let's pop over
to the Cloud console

00:19:52.094 --> 00:19:53.635
and walk through
some of our options.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.920
So the first thing you would
want to do in this scenario

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:03.690
is pop into the IAM
Service Accounts tab

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.430
and rotate that key.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:10.860
And you do that just by coming
over here to create a new key,

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:13.180
provision that out to
the system that needs it,

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:16.140
so that you can take this
without any downtime,

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:19.522
and then you delete the key
that we saw was compromised.

00:20:19.522 --> 00:20:20.730
I'm not going to do that yet.

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:21.990
For the sake of
demonstration, we're

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:23.906
actually going to walk
through what could have

00:20:23.906 --> 00:20:26.607
happened in this situation.

00:20:26.607 --> 00:20:28.440
So the next thing you
want to take a look at

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:32.370
is what permissions did that
service account actually have.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.570
So if you go into
the Permissions tab,

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:38.310
we can see that our CI/CD
pipeline service account here,

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.920
we granted it the
CI/CD Pipeline Role.

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:44.730
Now what that is actually
an IAM custom role.

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:47.700
So if I pop over to
the custom Roles tab,

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:51.400
you'll be able to see where
I defined the CI/CD Pipeline

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.560
Role.

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.500
And clicking into that, we see
that the only two permissions

00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:58.540
contained in that
role are the ability

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.450
to get deployments and the
ability to update deployments.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.580
We have a very
simplified CI/CD system.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.310
All it's doing is updating
the container version

00:21:06.310 --> 00:21:09.580
to point to the new
application we just built.

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:13.330
Now deployments.update is still
a fairly elevated permission.

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:15.340
It allows you, like
I just mentioned,

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.170
to change the container
image or to even

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.630
change parameters
or the environment

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:21.414
that your application
is running in.

00:21:21.414 --> 00:21:23.080
So we're not completely
out of the woods

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.780
yet, but, by limiting
these permissions

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:28.474
to exactly what this thing
needs to get its job done,

00:21:28.474 --> 00:21:29.890
we've at least
limited the surface

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:33.050
area of the potential attack.

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:37.660
So now that we've seen what this
thing could do, let's go ahead

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:40.870
and take a look at
what it has done.

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:44.520
So if we pop over to
the Stackdriver Logs,

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:47.070
and we look at the Kubernetes
logs, we see it's fairly noisy.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:49.050
The Kubernetes API is
a pretty noisy place.

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:51.000
There's a lot of
activity going on.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.730
Luckily, in this
Stackdriver UI, we

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:57.210
can easily filter
this via text search.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:00.690
We know our service
account had CI/CD in it.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:06.220
And we see that the thing
that's happened, so far,

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:07.630
is mostly what we expect.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:13.210
It's our hourly push
to our GKE cluster.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:14.290
So far, so good.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:19.180
We can dig in here, find
some interesting information

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:19.700
in this.

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:22.000
We can even start
streaming the logs

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.070
that we see new
activity as it happens.

00:22:26.854 --> 00:22:28.270
So let's switch
gears a little bit

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:29.770
and think about
what this might look

00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:34.520
like from a potential
attacker scenario.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:37.210
So a common way you
might see this happen

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:41.350
is an attacker might write
a very shallow, broad script

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:43.480
that's just scanning the
internet for anything that

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:45.490
looks like credentials.

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:48.640
Then you might take a
really quick attempt

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:50.920
at trying to make use
of those credentials,

00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:52.960
trying to make money
off that, probably,

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:56.410
by implanting a malicious
application somewhere.

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:58.030
So I've written
up a little script

00:22:58.030 --> 00:23:01.510
that's just going to pretend
to scan the internet for keys.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:04.420
So everything you see in
white is pretend stuff,

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:06.982
but the things that are in
green are actual commands,

00:23:06.982 --> 00:23:08.440
and then the things
that are in red

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:11.860
are the responses
from those commands.

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:13.870
So we're going to scan
the internet for keys.

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:17.890
And sure enough, it came across
our exposed service account key

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:20.052
for that CI/CD pipeline.

00:23:20.052 --> 00:23:21.760
So the next thing it
will do is configure

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:25.835
the shell to make use
of those credentials.

00:23:25.835 --> 00:23:27.460
And we're going to
go ahead and attempt

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:30.910
to implant our malicious
application as a Kubernetes

00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:31.930
pod in this cluster.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:38.070
And you'll see that, luckily,
that request was denied.

00:23:38.070 --> 00:23:40.760
So the error we got
back from Kubernetes

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:43.200
is that we did not have
enough permissions to make

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:44.490
this request.

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:47.190
If you remember back to the IAM
custom role we took a look at,

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:49.590
we had the ability to get
deployments and update

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:53.430
deployments, but we didn't have
the ability to create pods.

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:54.280
So that's good.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:58.320
So maybe the script tries
to create these things

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:02.440
a couple more times, but it's
going to fail every time.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:05.190
And if this is just a
shallow, cheap scan,

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:06.410
maybe it just moves on.

00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:10.999
And we were lucky that
we got safe there.

00:24:10.999 --> 00:24:13.040
Now, if you were watching
over here on the right,

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:17.550
you saw these logs
start to pop up.

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:21.830
And so, in this scenario,
it's fairly obvious

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:25.430
to see where the anomalous
activity came in from.

00:24:25.430 --> 00:24:28.160
In other incidents, it
may not be this easy.

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:31.700
We can directly see that this
double exclamation point,

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:33.260
red thing got an error.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:36.630
We can dig into how
exactly that happened.

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:40.220
But in real incidents,
you might have better luck

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:43.070
analyzing logs further,
here, or exporting them

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:46.880
to a different system to do
more in-depth processing.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:49.824
That's as easy as
creating an export.

00:24:49.824 --> 00:24:51.740
You can send that to
BigQuery, give it a name,

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:53.330
create the sync.

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:57.170
I've already done that, so let's
pop over to this BigQuery tab,

00:24:57.170 --> 00:25:00.380
where I have streamed logs
to a BigQuery data set.

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:02.780
I've already
written out a query.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:05.960
I'm going to take a look
for any calls that have

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:09.080
come from this service account.

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:10.640
But they came from
outside what I'm

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:12.920
going to pretend is
our corporate IP range.

00:25:12.920 --> 00:25:16.100
We know our CI/CD
system is only going

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.650
to be calling from this
IP range that we expect,

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:21.590
so if we do see accesses from
this service account coming

00:25:21.590 --> 00:25:23.600
from elsewhere,
we'd like to know.

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:24.512
That's unusual.

00:25:24.512 --> 00:25:25.970
And then, just from
that, I'm going

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:29.640
to gather some
pertinent information.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:32.090
So we take a look at this query.

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:37.102
And sure enough, it's going to
pop up with those three calls

00:25:37.102 --> 00:25:38.700
that that script just made.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:42.630
We see, from the HTTP code,
our attacker was unsuccessful,

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:44.490
didn't have enough permissions.

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.710
And we have the
IP of the caller,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:50.580
right here, that can help inform
some of our additional incident

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:52.450
response.

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:54.870
So I'm not going to go
any deeper into incident

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:55.980
response here.

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:58.287
Incidents are fairly unique.

00:25:58.287 --> 00:26:00.120
I just wanted to highlight
some of the tools

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:03.870
that we've provided that
help you start to begin

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:05.566
your incident response.

00:26:05.566 --> 00:26:07.440
If you've ever been in
a situation like this,

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:09.420
you know how critical
it can be to get

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:14.280
good, solid information quickly
to stakeholders and leadership.

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:18.300
So we walked through how
GKE's integration with Google

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.220
authentication gives
you a single place

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:23.700
to go manage and rotate
your credentials.

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:28.440
We saw how GKE works with IAM to
grant granular least privilege

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:32.550
permissions and set and
enforce that across your org.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:37.360
And then lastly, we saw how
GKE's automatic audit logging

00:26:37.360 --> 00:26:40.980
gives you the ability to have
a solid incident response

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:44.775
story without having to have
remembered to turn it on.

00:26:44.775 --> 00:26:46.650
And with that, I'm going
to hand it back over

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:49.230
to Greg, who's going to
talk through some real life

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:51.625
examples of vulnerability
management in GKE.

00:26:56.973 --> 00:26:58.280
GREG CASTLE: Thanks, CJ.

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:00.980
So I wanted to talk a little
about vulnerability management.

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:03.620
It's something that often
doesn't get a lot of air time.

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:07.530
And I wanted to do that with
some example case studies.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:09.950
Now these case studies are
just two examples in time.

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:11.540
I think they're
both interesting,

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:14.390
but not every vulnerability is
going to work out like this.

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:16.700
Each vulnerability tends
to be unique on a number

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:18.570
of different axes.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:22.165
So with that caveat, the
first one I want to talk about

00:27:22.165 --> 00:27:23.540
is some vulnerabilities
that were

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:27.890
found in some software called
Dnsmasq, back in October,

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:29.240
in 2017.

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:30.980
So this is open source software.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:34.740
It is a very well-used piece of
software not written by Google.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.500
It's open source.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:40.220
And it provides critical DNS
services and other networking

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:43.410
services for many, many
different companies.

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:45.140
It's very well-used.

00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:49.230
It runs on laptops, desktops,
servers, IoT devices,

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:51.450
lots of different places.

00:27:51.450 --> 00:27:54.380
So I'm going to step through
how the vulnerability was found,

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:57.860
how we fixed it, and what
the outcome was for GKE.

00:27:57.860 --> 00:28:00.180
So how was this
vulnerability found?

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:03.290
So Google employs a bunch of
really world-class security

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:04.880
researchers, internally.

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:07.820
They are focused on
finding vulnerabilities

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:09.470
in our own products
and fixing them,

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:11.090
as well as finding
vulnerabilities

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:14.060
in critical pieces of
internet infrastructure

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:16.230
and making the internet safer.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:17.300
So in this case--

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:20.030
oh, actually, and we also
have the Vulnerability Rewards

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:22.610
Program, where we engage the
external security research

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:25.700
community and we pay
them for their work

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:28.080
through a bug bounty program.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.110
So in this case,
for Dnsmasq, this

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:32.270
was a Google-discovered
vulnerability.

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.490
So Google security researcher
found a whole series

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:35.952
of vulnerabilities, actually.

00:28:35.952 --> 00:28:37.910
And if you're interested
in kind of geeking out

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:39.950
on the details of
these vulnerabilities,

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:42.380
you can go read all
about them here.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:45.440
It's not really super-relevant
for this presentation,

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:50.360
but the worst of them was
a remote code execution.

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:52.880
So the Google
security researchers

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.430
started working with the
maintainer of Dnsmasq, who's

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:58.010
an external party,
doesn't work at Google.

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:00.440
Together they came
up with a fix that

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:02.670
fixed the vulnerabilities
that had been found.

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:06.200
And then we started a
worldwide multi-company

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:07.940
coordinated disclosure effort.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:12.890
This was really complicated
disclosures to try and get

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:15.320
as many people
patched as possible.

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:19.640
And we enlisted the help of
the US CERT, the US Computer

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:22.620
Emergency Readiness
Team, with that effort.

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:25.560
So what did this mean for GKE?

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:28.950
So internally, we identified
that GKE and Kubernetes

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:31.680
depended on Dnsmasq.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:36.160
And that meant that we
knew we were vulnerable.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:40.200
And so we kicked off the
Kubernetes security response

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:44.460
process, and that starts with us
emailing the Kubernetes product

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:47.500
security team, which we
actually sit on as well, too.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:51.900
So that's a handful of
representatives from companies,

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:54.200
Google, Microsoft, Red Hat.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:57.670
In this case, CJ actually led
the vulnerability response,

00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:59.203
so we volunteered
to take the lead.

00:29:59.203 --> 00:30:00.836
In other cases, it
might be someone

00:30:00.836 --> 00:30:02.460
from Microsoft or
someone from Red Hat,

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:07.590
all very experienced security
engineers with deep expertise

00:30:07.590 --> 00:30:09.390
in Kubernetes.

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:12.720
So we took the lead on this
one, which was, essentially,

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:15.270
working out how to
fix it in Kubernetes.

00:30:15.270 --> 00:30:17.190
So we figured out
that we had to patch

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.710
kube DNS with this new fix
that had been developed

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:23.610
with the Dnsmasq maintainer,
and release a new version

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:25.920
of that container.

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:31.380
And once we'd gotten
the fix kind of settled,

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:35.160
we had to build and stage
quite a number of releases.

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:38.550
So the releases for the
security release process,

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:42.780
which is extensively documented
at this link-- you can go

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:44.400
read all about it.

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:46.830
But essentially, what
we were able to do

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:50.580
was create Kubernetes releases
for three different release

00:30:50.580 --> 00:30:52.290
branches, and even,
actually, one more,

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:56.310
because 1.8 was about to
be released at the time.

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:58.290
All of those contained--
we were able to patch

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:00.630
all those branches,
with the fix and release

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:04.950
the fix on the same day as the
public vulnerability went out.

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:08.910
And for GKE, this meant that,
as part of the managed service,

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:11.970
we were able to make a change
on the masters that rolled out

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:14.970
a change to kube DNS
across the clusters.

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:17.010
And so the outcome
for GKE customers

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.375
was that we were able to
patch this vulnerability,

00:31:19.375 --> 00:31:22.650
at the public release time,
without any action required

00:31:22.650 --> 00:31:24.160
on the part of the customer.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:26.720
So I think that's kind
of the ideal outcome,

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:29.820
that Google spends a whole bunch
of engineering hours figuring

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:32.820
out how to do all this and
just fixes it for everyone.

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:34.570
And you don't even
have to worry about it.

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.870
You may not have even been
aware of this vulnerability.

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:38.500
It won't always
work out like that.

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:42.120
But I think that
is the ideal case.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:44.820
The second case study I
wanted to talk about briefly

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:48.660
is the Spectre and
Meltdown vulnerabilities.

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:53.070
So this was from early
January this year.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:56.104
So these were very
interesting vulnerabilities.

00:31:56.104 --> 00:31:57.770
Probably most of the
people in this room

00:31:57.770 --> 00:31:59.630
have heard about them.

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:01.310
If you haven't,
they were discovered

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:02.510
by Google Project Zero.

00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:05.300
There were vulnerabilities
in most modern CPUs.

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:08.390
They're caused by the
speculative execution feature.

00:32:08.390 --> 00:32:10.730
I'm also not going to dig
in to the details of this.

00:32:10.730 --> 00:32:13.790
If you do want details,
there's an entire Next session

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:15.590
that has actually
already happened,

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:17.210
but you can find it on YouTube.

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:18.877
If you want to dig
into how Google found

00:32:18.877 --> 00:32:20.668
these vulnerabilities
and how we fixed them

00:32:20.668 --> 00:32:22.400
across the whole
infrastructure, that

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:24.350
should be a really
interesting talk.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:25.430
There's also a blog post.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:26.929
And if you just
Google these things,

00:32:26.929 --> 00:32:28.400
you'll find out
a ton about this.

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:31.260
They were very well-known.

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:33.450
So what did this mean for GKE?

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.630
So we knew that we had to
make virtual machine changes.

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:40.800
So there was changes
necessary to the hypervisor

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:45.960
to prevent one VM accessing
the host memory of the hardware

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:48.150
machine that was running
the VM and getting access

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:50.320
to another VM on
the same machine.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:54.386
So we needed some hypervisor
changes to fix that problem.

00:32:54.386 --> 00:32:56.010
And then, as a second
layer of defense,

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:57.760
we also needed some
changes in the kernel,

00:32:57.760 --> 00:32:59.910
enabling some new
protections there.

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:02.610
That required a
new version of COS.

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:06.990
So that was essentially a
second layer of protection,

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:09.270
and you'll hear Google's
security engineers

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.190
talk about two layers
of defense a lot.

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:13.770
So we're always looking
for two layers of defense

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:15.210
where we can find them.

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:17.160
It doesn't always happen,
but our experience

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:21.086
has told us that, when we can
get two layers of defense,

00:33:21.086 --> 00:33:22.210
that's a really good thing.

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:24.570
And it really helps.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:28.980
So there was lots of testing
involved, many, many hours

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:32.220
of engineering time spent
on getting these fixes

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:34.740
and testing them,
testing upgrade paths,

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:36.210
testing performance
in particular,

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:38.070
because these new
kernel security

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:41.850
features had some significant
performance changes.

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:46.690
So the outcome for GKE, I
think, was actually pretty cool.

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:50.520
So we were able to
live-migrate using technology

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:53.490
that GCE basically
built. So we were

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:57.210
able to take those nodes that
were running customer workloads

00:33:57.210 --> 00:34:00.540
and live-migrate them onto that
new version of that hypervisor

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.040
without any interruption.

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:03.930
I think that's actually
pretty awesome,

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:05.760
given the scale of
these vulnerabilities

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.890
and how complex they were.

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:10.199
There was still some
customer work required.

00:34:10.199 --> 00:34:14.489
So that upgrade to the COS image
did require a node upgrade,

00:34:14.489 --> 00:34:17.370
like I mentioned
previously, but we

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:21.090
were able to deliver that first
defense without any customer

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:24.010
effort required.

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:26.300
So in general, how do
we handle communications

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:28.650
for security patches
and similar issues?

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:31.230
If there is a serious
security vulnerability,

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:34.500
we'll send targeted emails
if it requires some action

00:34:34.500 --> 00:34:35.924
by the customer.

00:34:35.924 --> 00:34:38.340
And for everything else, we
have a security bulletins page

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:39.730
that you can see at this link.

00:34:39.730 --> 00:34:43.199
And it has a RSS feed you
can also subscribe to.

00:34:43.199 --> 00:34:46.750
All these links are
at the end, too.

00:34:46.750 --> 00:34:48.900
I also briefly wanted to
mention, again, the Google

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:50.670
Vulnerabilities Reward Program.

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:53.100
So we've built on a lot of
success, with this program,

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:57.087
with Android, Chrome, and all
of Google's web properties.

00:34:57.087 --> 00:34:58.920
And I just wanted to
call out, specifically,

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:01.250
that it does apply to GKE.

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:03.486
This is not a new announcement.

00:35:03.486 --> 00:35:06.000
It has applied for
a very long time.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:09.270
But this essentially gives
us a force multiplier

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:12.720
for engaging that external
security research community

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:15.521
and getting vulnerabilities,
finding new vulnerabilities

00:35:15.521 --> 00:35:17.145
and fixing them as
quickly as possible.

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:21.730
So before I hand
it back to Aaron,

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:24.470
I just wanted to mention the
key takeaway, from that section,

00:35:24.470 --> 00:35:26.720
I think, is that Google is
doing a whole bunch of work

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:29.765
in vulnerability management to
make it as easy as possible,

00:35:29.765 --> 00:35:31.740
for you, as the customer.

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:34.280
It won't always be zero
effort on your behalf,

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:36.740
but we're really trying to
minimize that effort, that you

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:39.980
need to put in, on those kind
of annoying problems of patching

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:41.124
vulnerabilities.

00:35:50.815 --> 00:35:52.440
AARON SMALL: OK, so
I feel like we just

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:54.180
shotgunned a lot of
content at you guys,

00:35:54.180 --> 00:35:57.120
so I'm going to give
a pretty brief recap.

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:58.620
Greg discussed the
layered approach

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:01.920
that we take to GKE
security, all the way from VM

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:05.060
provisioning up to
Kubernetes configuration.

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:07.440
CJ discussed control
plane security

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:10.350
as well as gave us
a demo of how you

00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:13.800
might handle the compromise
of a sensitive secret.

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:15.480
And then Greg came
back and walked us

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:17.313
through a couple of
marquee vulnerabilities,

00:36:17.313 --> 00:36:20.880
over the last year or so,
and how we managed them.

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:22.760
So what's the point
of all of this?

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:24.140
What message are we sending?

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:25.970
What do we want
you to take away?

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:29.900
That's that software security
is our full time job on GKE.

00:36:29.900 --> 00:36:32.480
And we want to give to you
that trustworthy infrastructure

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:34.910
you need to build
state of the art, world

00:36:34.910 --> 00:36:36.860
changing secure applications.

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:41.410
This is one of the photo slides.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:45.490
We have a ton of great container
security content at Next.

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:47.090
Some of this has
already happened,

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:48.610
so it will be
available on YouTube.

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:51.440
I would highly recommend
going back and watching them.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:54.159
We also have several
more talks today

00:36:54.159 --> 00:36:56.200
that I would definitely
encourage you to go visit

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:58.010
if you care about security.

00:36:58.010 --> 00:37:01.660
I'll be at most
of them, if I can.

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:06.131
And this is the links slide
that Greg referenced before.

00:37:06.131 --> 00:37:08.910
Glad it's big enough
for you to read.

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:09.680
Thank you.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.740
That is all the content
that we have so far.

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:15.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

